+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Run OnionShare as your ordinary user, not root. It starts the system Tor
binary with private state and a Unix control socket; do not enable tor's
rc.d service solely for OnionShare. Tor is not embedded in this package.

Bridge transports are optional. Install the appropriate transport and
configure bridges explicitly if required. Missing transports must cause
bridge setup to fail, not bypass the requested bridge. Built-in bridge
addresses and Tor transport interoperability need runtime verification.

Chat uses the system Flask-SocketIO 5, python-socketio 5, python-engineio 4,
gevent and gevent-websocket packages. The port requires gevent explicitly,
so Eventlet is neither installed nor selected as a fallback. This preserves
the chat client's WebSocket-only transport rather than falling back silently
to an incompatible backend.

The port metadata accepts the OpenBSD versions of PyNaCl, Werkzeug, Stem and
qrcode after static API review. CFFI remains installed transitively through
PyNaCl and gevent. Click, urllib3, setuptools, wheel, Cython and pkgconfig
are not imported by OnionShare at runtime; urllib3 and wsproto remain
transitive dependencies of requests and simple-websocket respectively.

The Engine.IO port also retains requests and websocket-client so its packaged
outbound client remains usable. OnionShare's Flask-SocketIO server does not
import those client paths directly.

This update is untested on OpenBSD. In particular test all five modes,
authenticated/public sharing, symbolic-link handling, Unix-socket and TCP
fallbacks, Tor shutdown and cleanup, bridge transports, WebSocket chat, and
GUI/PySide6 compatibility before relying on it for privacy.
